The same handler, written sequentially and concurrently.

```csharp
// Sequential: each await finishes before the next call starts.
public async Task<TicketView> LoadSequential(string id, CancellationToken ct)
{
    var ticket   = await _tickets.GetAsync(id, ct);      // 40 ms
    var comments = await _comments.ForTicketAsync(id, ct); // 60 ms
    var customer = await _customers.GetAsync(ticket.CustomerId, ct); // 30 ms
    return new TicketView(ticket, comments, customer);   // ~130 ms total
}

// Concurrent: start the independent work, then await together.
public async Task<TicketView> LoadConcurrent(string id, CancellationToken ct)
{
    var ticketTask   = _tickets.GetAsync(id, ct);
    var commentsTask = _comments.ForTicketAsync(id, ct);

    await Task.WhenAll(ticketTask, commentsTask);        // ~60 ms so far

    var ticket = await ticketTask;                       // already complete
    var customer = await _customers.GetAsync(ticket.CustomerId, ct); // must follow
    return new TicketView(ticket, await commentsTask, customer);
}
```

Note what could *not* be parallelised: the customer lookup needs `ticket.CustomerId`, so it genuinely depends on the first call and has to wait. Fan-out applies only to independent work, and pretending a dependency does not exist is how concurrency bugs start.

And the blocking anti-pattern, for recognition:

```csharp
var ticket = _tickets.GetAsync(id, ct).Result;   // blocks a thread; can deadlock
```

items.promptMarkdown:
The dashboard endpoint takes about 300 ms. Each of the three calls takes about 100 ms and none of them uses another's result.

```csharp
public async Task<Dashboard> LoadAsync(string ownerId, CancellationToken ct)
{
    var open     = await _tickets.CountOpenAsync(ownerId, ct);
    var breached = await _tickets.CountBreachedAsync(ownerId, ct);
    var recent   = await _tickets.RecentAsync(ownerId, ct);

    return new Dashboard(open, breached, recent);
}
```

The code is correct. What should change, and what would the new timing be?

solution.modelAnswer:
The three calls are independent, but each `await` suspends the method until that call finishes, so they run one after another and the durations add up. Start them all first and then await together:

```csharp
var openTask     = _tickets.CountOpenAsync(ownerId, ct);
var breachedTask = _tickets.CountBreachedAsync(ownerId, ct);
var recentTask   = _tickets.RecentAsync(ownerId, ct);

await Task.WhenAll(openTask, breachedTask, recentTask);

return new Dashboard(await openTask, await breachedTask, await recentTask);
```

The three now overlap, so the endpoint takes roughly 100 ms — the slowest call — rather than 300 ms.

