Clients report that abandoning a slow export does not reduce database load. Every method accepts a token.

```csharp
// 1
public async Task<IActionResult> Export(string ownerId, CancellationToken ct)
    => Ok(await _export.BuildAsync(ownerId, ct));

// 2
public async Task<Report> BuildAsync(string ownerId, CancellationToken ct)
{
    var rows = await _repo.AllRowsAsync(ownerId, ct);
    foreach (var row in rows)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        Accumulate(row);
    }
    return Finish();
}

// 3
public Task<List<Row>> AllRowsAsync(string ownerId, CancellationToken ct)
    => _db.Rows.Where(r => r.OwnerId == ownerId).ToListAsync();
```

Which layer breaks the chain, and why does the loop's check not rescue it?

solution.code:
function firstBrokenLink(chain) {
  for (let i = 0; i < chain.length; i = i + 1) {
    const layer = chain[i];
    const isLast = i === chain.length - 1;
    if (layer.acceptsToken !== true) {
      return layer.name;
    }
    if (!isLast && layer.forwardsToken !== true) {
      return layer.name;
    }
  }
  return 'intact';
}

grading.starterCode:
function firstBrokenLink(chain) {
  // Only inspects the final layer.
  if (chain.length === 0) {
    return 'intact';
  }
  const last = chain[chain.length - 1];
  if (last.acceptsToken !== true) {
    return last.name;
  }
  return 'intact';
}

items.workedExampleMarkdown:
Three ways a failure can disappear, and the fix for each.

```csharp
// 1. Nobody awaits it: the exception is stored and never seen.
_ = _mailer.SendReceiptAsync(order);        // fails silently, forever

// Observe it deliberately if it must be fire-and-forget:
_ = _mailer.SendReceiptAsync(order)
    .ContinueWith(t => _log.LogError(t.Exception, "receipt send failed"),
                  TaskContinuationOptions.OnlyOnFaulted);

// 2. WhenAll: three failures, one rethrown.
var all = Task.WhenAll(a, b, c);
try
{
    await all;
}
catch (Exception)
{
    // all.Exception holds every failure; the await surfaced only the first
    foreach (var inner in all.Exception!.InnerExceptions)
    {
        _log.LogError(inner, "batch member failed");
    }
    throw;
}

// 3. A try around a call that is not awaited.
try
{
    _ = ProcessAsync(order);   // returns immediately; exception arrives later
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    // never reached
}
```

The second pattern is the one most worth adopting as a habit, because the failure mode is so plausible: a batch of ten uploads, three of them fail, you fix the first cause, redeploy, and discover the second. Capturing the aggregate turns three deployments into one.

